<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jalón</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Jalón is a river of north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, a right-bank tributary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ebro-river">Ebro</a> that rises in the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soria">Soria</a> and flows north-east past <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calatayud">Calatayud</a> to join the Ebro near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaragoza">Zaragoza</a>. Its valley carries the main route between the Ebro basin and the central Meseta, followed by the Roman road and by today's railway and motorway. In antiquity it was the land of Celtiberian peoples such as the Titti, Belli and Lusones.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jalon.jpg" alt="Jalón" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ecelan · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Urrea_de_Jal%C3%B3n_-_Ayuntamiento.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Unlike many conquerors, the kings let much of the Muslim population stay on the land, and the mudéjares, Muslims living under Christian rule, remained a large part of the rural workforce on the estates of nobles and the military orders, especially in the Ebro and <b>Jalón</b> valleys.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-aragon">Kingdom of Aragon · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Belli lived in the <b>Jalón</b> and Huerva valleys of modern Zaragoza province from about the 4th century BCE, part of the wider Celtiberian world.</blockquote><a href="/state/belli">Belli</a></li><li><blockquote>The Lusones lived on the high plateau around the sources of the Tagus and the <b>Jalón</b>, between modern Guadalajara and Zaragoza.</blockquote><a href="/state/lusones">Lusones</a></li><li><blockquote>Muhammad received Calatayud, an old town on the <b>Jalón</b> controlling the road from Zaragoza to Toledo.</blockquote><a href="/state/taifa-of-calatayud">Taifa of Calatayud</a></li><li><blockquote>The Titti were one of the Celtiberian peoples of the middle Ebro tributaries, probably around the <b>Jalón</b> valley in modern Zaragoza province.</blockquote><a href="/state/titti">Titti</a></li></ul></section>
